# Defect 453's macro fixture read the other way (panel 238's R4): the module
# whose header defines the macro `twice`, `hundred`, is named to be walked
# first, so the one file `--emit-c` writes reads its header before the
# binding's. `build` prints 300 and 8; the one file is refused, since the
# macro rewrites the binding's own declaration into C clang refuses. Until
# defect 2369 it was told in clang's words at `hundred`'s group, where the
# other order (`onefile453macro/`) names the binding the macro rewrites;
# both orders now name it, so the modules' names never choose the message.
use hundred
use narrow

function main()
    print(hundred.hundredfold(x: 3))
    print(narrow.doubled(x: 4))
